{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a85a73e3ba8f82ce", "type": "tool_result", "content": "<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (30KB). Full output saved to: /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io/.claude-review/projects/-inspire-qb-ilm-project-video-generation-chenxie-25019-hyr-iHateTheWorld555-github-io/48b42dfc-bdbe-4536-b968-6ad02f381531/tool-results/bbafd0j5f.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n Deep Learning Based Relative Transfer Matrix Estimation for Multiple Sources and Multiple Microphones Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Submit without GitHub Submit in GitHub arXiv is now an independent nonprofit! Learn more × Back to arXiv Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Abstract 1 Introduction 2 Problem formulation 3 Proposed ReTM estimation models 3.1 STFT convolutional network (SCoNet) 3.2 Convolutional filter and summation network (FuSNet) 3.3 LSTM based autoencoder network (LAeNet) 3.4 Loss function and training 4 Experiments 4.1 Experimental methodology 4.2 Results and discussion 5 Application into speech enhancement 6 Conclusion 7 Acknowledgements 8 Use of Generative AI Disclosure References License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2608.11627v1 [eess.AS] 12 Aug 2026 Yalegama Manamperi Deep Learning Based Relative Transfer Matrix Estimation for Multiple Sources and Multiple Microphones Oshan A. B Wageesha N Abstract The Relative Transfer Matrix (ReTM), recently introduced as a generalization of the relative transfer function for multiple receivers and sources, shows promising performance when applied to speech enhancement in noisy environments. Estimating the ReTM of sound sources by exploiting the covariance matrices of multichannel recordings is highly beneficial for practical applications and, to date, remains the only proposed approach. This paper investigates deep learning-based ReTM estimation. We propose